          "content": "Perfect! Now I have a complete picture. Let me provide a clear explanation:\n\n---\n\n## **Remote Connection in Jupyter-k8s**\n\nRemote connection refers to **connecting to a workspace from a desktop IDE** (like VS Code) rather than through a web browser. This enables developers to use their local IDE while the code executes in the Kubernetes workspace.\n\n### **How It Works**\n\n#### **1. Connection Types**\n\nThere are two distinct connection methods:\n\n**A. Web UI Connection** (`web-ui`)\n- Access JupyterLab through a web browser\n- Uses HTTPS ingress with OIDC authentication\n- Standard web-based workflow\n\n**B. VS Code Remote Connection** (`vscode-remote`)\n- Connect VS Code desktop app to the workspace\n- Uses **AWS Systems Manager (SSM)** for secure tunneling\n- **No direct SSH ingress** - all traffic tunneled through SSM\n\n#### **2. SSM Remote Access Architecture**\n\nThe remote connection uses AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to create secure tunnels:\n\n```\nVS Code Desktop → SSM Session → SSM Agent Sidecar → Remote Access Server (port 2222) → Workspace Container\n```\n\n**Key Components:**\n\n1. **SSM Agent Sidecar Container**\n   - Injected into workspace pods via `WorkspaceAccessStrategy`\n   - Runs AWS SSM agent to register as a managed instance\n   - Shares `/opt/amazon/sagemaker/workspace` volume with main container\n\n2. **Remote Access Server**\n   - Runs on port 2222 inside the workspace container\n   - Handles SSH-like connections from VS Code\n   - Started by script: `/opt/amazon/sagemaker/workspace/remote-access/start-remote-access-server.sh`\n\n3. **SSM Activation & Registration**\n   - Controller creates SSM activation codes via AWS API\n   - Sidecar registers as a managed instance using activation code\n   - Tagged with workspace metadata (pod UID, workspace name, namespace)\n\n#### **3. Connection Flow**\n\n**Step 1: User Requests Connection**\n```bash\nPOST /apis/connection.workspace.jupyter.org/v1alpha1/namespaces/{ns}/workspaceconnections\n{\n  \"spec\": {\n    \"workspaceName\": \"my-workspace\",\n    \"workspaceConnectionType\": \"vscode-remote\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Step 2: Extension API Validates & Generates URL**\n- Checks workspace is available (status condition)\n- Verifies SSM is configured in `WorkspaceAccessStrategy`\n- Retrieves pod UID from workspace deployment\n- Finds SSM managed instance by pod UID tag\n\n**Step 3: Start SSM Session**\n```go\n// Calls AWS SSM StartSession API\nsessionInfo, err := ssmClient.StartSession(ctx, instanceID, documentName, \"2222\")\n```\n\nReturns:\n- `sessionId`: Unique session identifier\n- `tokenValue`: Authentication token\n- `streamUrl`: WebSocket URL for SSM tunnel\n\n**Step 4: Generate VS Code URL**\n```\nvscode://amazonwebservices.aws-toolkit-vscode/connect/workspace?\n  sessionId=<session-id>&\n  sessionToken=<token>&\n  streamUrl=<stream-url>&\n  workspaceName=<name>&\n  namespace=<ns>&\n  eksClusterArn=<arn>\n```\n\n**Step 5: VS Code Opens Connection**\n- AWS Toolkit extension handles the `vscode://` URL scheme\n- Establishes SSM session using provided credentials\n- Tunnels SSH-like traffic through SSM to port 2222\n- VS Code Remote connects to the remote access server\n\n#### **4. Sidecar Injection via AccessStrategy**\n\nThe `WorkspaceAccessStrategy` defines deployment modifications:\n\n```yaml\ndeploymentModifications:\n  podModifications:\n    additionalContainers:\n      - name: ssm-agent-sidecar\n        image: <ssm-sidecar-image>\n        command: [/opt/amazon/sagemaker/workspace/bin/entrypoint-workspace-sidecar-container]\n        volumeMounts:\n          - name: workspace-base\n            mountPath: /opt/amazon/sagemaker/workspace\n        readinessProbe:\n          exec:\n            command: [\"test\", \"-f\", \"/tmp/ssm-registered\"]\n    \n    volumes:\n      - name: workspace-base\n        emptyDir: {}\n    \n    primaryContainerModifications:\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: workspace-base\n          mountPath: /opt/amazon/sagemaker/workspace\n```\n\n#### **5. SSM Registration Process**\n\nWhen a workspace pod starts:\n\n1. **Init Container** copies SSM agent binaries to shared volume\n2. **Sidecar Container** starts and runs registration script\n3. **Controller watches pod events** and triggers setup:\n   ```go\n   SetupContainers(ctx, pod, workspace, accessStrategy)\n   ```\n4. **Creates SSM Activation** via AWS API with tags:\n   - `workspace-pod-uid`: Links to specific pod\n   - `workspace-name`, `namespace`: Metadata\n5. **Executes registration** in sidecar via `kubectl exec`:\n   ```bash\n   amazon-ssm-agent -register -code <activation-code> -id <activation-id> -region <region>\n   ```\n6. **Marks ready** by creating `/tmp/ssm-registered` file\n\n#### **6. Security Features**\n\n- **No SSH Ingress**: No direct network access to workspaces\n- **IAM-Based**: SSM uses IAM roles for authentication\n- **Temporary Sessions**: Sessions expire after use\n- **Audit Trail**: All SSM sessions logged in CloudTrail\n- **Owner Verification**: Extension API checks workspace ownership before generating URLs\n- **Pod UID Tagging**: Ensures connections only to correct pod instances\n\n#### **7. Configuration Requirements**\n\n**In WorkspaceAccessStrategy:**\n```yaml\ncreateConnectionContext:\n  ssmManagedNodeRole: \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/SSMManagedNodeRole\"\n  ssmDocumentName: \"AWS-StartPortForwardingSession\"\n```\n\n**Environment Variables:**\n- `CLUSTER_ID`: EKS cluster ARN (required for connection URLs)\n- Controller needs AWS credentials with SSM permissions\n\n**IAM Permissions:**\n- `ssm:CreateActivation`\n- `ssm:StartSession`\n- `ssm:DescribeInstanceInformation`\n- `ssm:DeleteActivation`\n- `ssm:DeregisterManagedInstance`\n\n### **Why This Approach?**\n\n1. **Security**: No SSH ports exposed, all traffic through AWS-managed tunnels\n2. **Simplicity**: No need for bastion hosts or VPN\n3. **Auditability**: CloudTrail logs all connection attempts\n4. **Multi-tenancy**: Each workspace isolated, no shared SSH keys\n5. **Cloud-native**: Leverages AWS infrastructure for connectivity\n\nThis is essentially **\"SSH over SSM\"** - providing remote IDE access without traditional SSH ingress, making it suitable for secure, multi-tenant Kubernetes environments."
